=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r11
diagnosis_text:
The OSPF↔EIGRP redistribution boundary is entirely handled by r11, which participates in both OSPF (area 3, via 111.0.0.0/8 toward r1) and EIGRP AS 100 (via 112.0.0.0/8 toward r12 and 103.0.0.0/8 toward r13). Two-way redistribution requires a `redistribute eigrp` statement inside the OSPF process AND a `redistribute ospf` statement inside the EIGRP process. r11's running configuration has only the first half: the OSPF stanza carries `redistribute eigrp` (and `redistribute connected`), but the EIGRP stanza contains only `network` declarations with no `redistribute ospf`.

The practical effect is strictly one-directional leaking. The EIGRP→OSPF direction works: r1's routing table confirms it receives `O>* 120.0.0.0/8 [110/20] via 111.1.1.2` and `O>* 102.1.x.0/24` — all EIGRP prefixes redistributed by r11 into OSPF. The reverse, OSPF→EIGRP, is completely absent: r11's EIGRP topology table holds zero redistributed entries, and both r12 and r13 have no OSPF-domain routes whatsoever (the OSPF space covering 1.x through 111.x is entirely invisible to the EIGRP routers). r12 survives only because it has a kernel default route (`K>* 0.0.0.0/0`) pointing back to r11.

Root cause: the `redistribute ospf` command was removed from (or never added to) r11's `router eigrp 100` process, breaking the OSPF→EIGRP half of the redistribution.

Corrective fix: on r11, enter `router eigrp 100` and add `redistribute ospf metric 10000 100 255 1 1500` (adjust the five-part EIGRP seed metric to match site policy). This will immediately inject all OSPF-learned prefixes into the EIGRP topology and propagate them to r12 and r13, restoring full bidirectional reachability.
=== END ===